@node Booleans
@section Booleans

@cindex true
@cindex false

The standard boolean objects for true and false are written as @code{#t}
and @code{#f}.  Alternatively, they can be written @code{#true} and
@code{#false}, respectively.  What really matters, though, are the
objects that the Scheme conditional ex@value{shy}pres@value{shy}sions
(@code{if}, @code{cond}, @code{and}, @code{or}, @code{when},
@code{unless}, @code{do}) treat as true or false.  The phrase
@quotedbl{a true value} (or sometimes just @quotedbl{true}) means any
object treated as true by the conditional expressions, and the phrase
@quotedbl{a false value} (or @quotedbl{false}) means any object treated
as false by the conditional expressions.

Of all the Scheme values, only @code{#f} counts as false in conditional
expressions.  All other Scheme values, including @code{#t}, count as
true.

@cindex empty list

@quotation
@i{Note:} Unlike some other dialects of Lisp, Scheme distinguishes
@code{#f} and the empty list empty list from each other and from the
symbol @code{nil}.
@end quotation

Boolean constants evaluate to themselves, so they do not need to be
quoted in programs.

@lisp
#t  @ok{#t}
#f  @ok{#f}
'#f @ok{#f}
@end lisp

@deffn procedure not obj

The @code{not} procedure returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is false, and
returns @code{#f} otherwise.

@lisp
(not #t)       @ok{#f}
(not 3)        @ok{#f}
(not (list 3)) @ok{#f}
(not #f)       @ok{#t}
(not '())      @ok{#f}
(not (list))   @ok{#f}
(not 'nil)     @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure boolean? obj

The @code{boolean?} predicate returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is either
@code{#t} or @code{#f} and returns @code{#f} otherwise.

@lisp
(boolean? #f)  @ok{#t}
(boolean? 0)   @ok{#f}
(boolean? '()) @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure boolean=? @pari{boolean} @parii{boolean} @pariii{boolean} @value{defdots}

Returns @code{#t} if all the arguments are
@code{#t} or all are @code{#f}.

@end deffn
